# dataset/.gitattributes
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS: byte-exact provenance on Windows.
#
# Git on Windows normally has core.autocrlf=true, which rewrites LF to CRLF in
# the working tree and back on commit for anything it decides is "text". The
# acquisition manifests in this folder record a sha256 of the ORIGINAL DOWNLOADED
# BYTES:
#
#   raw/dengue/_manifest.csv    sha256 per PDF, 1,639 rows
#   raw/weather/_manifest.csv   sha256 per NASA POWER JSON response, 64 rows
#
# The weather files are JSON, which git treats as text. Without this file, a
# fresh clone on another machine would receive different bytes than were
# downloaded, every recorded sha256 would fail to verify, and the provenance
# claim in SOURCES.md rule 2 would quietly become false — with nothing failing
# loudly to say so.
#
# Marking raw/ as -text disables all end-of-line conversion there, so the bytes
# on disk after a clone are the bytes that were fetched.
raw/** -text

# Generated outputs are regenerable from the scripts, so normalisation is
# harmless for them and left at git's default.
